<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/10th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Dorostolon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">971</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Siege of Dorostolon (971) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslavs-invasion-of-bulgaria">Sviatoslav's invasion of Bulgaria</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of Dorostolon took place from April to July 971 at Dorostolon, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silistra">Silistra</a>, on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> besieged Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a>, who had invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, until hunger and losses forced him to make peace and withdraw. The war ended Rus' ambitions on the Danube and led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>'s annexation of eastern Bulgaria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-dorostolon.jpg" alt="Siege of Dorostolon" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henryk Siemiradzki · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BelagerungvonDorostolon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
